Interfaces should ask for as little attention as possible throughout the user's life. Choices can be done quickly. The best interface is Interfaceless.
Built-in UI is minimal, not taking screenspace from users and flexible, fitting many Needs. Full control is always available.
Components are flat by default, preffering 2D forces us to convey information only with flat data, not layers and composition, constraining the space forces minimalism.
Shadows and hints can be used for subtle differentiation of areas, components and data.
Built-in castles shoudld be focused in displaying mainly inputs or buttons, never phrases or paragraphs, to force self-explanatory design.
Tooltips and placeholders should contain minimal text.
The defaults are flat, quiet and mostly black and white, with the purple palette already in the Record.
 Common choices should be quick, and the person should be able to inspect and change the details.
 Sands: the widgets/components that live in the Box. Smaller pieces can be composed into groups, with interactions between them and other sands, receiving data from a Protein stream and executing Protein Actions in certain interactions. We should have the fundamental blocks to construct several types of workflows. Protein supplies authorized reads. Backend Actions remain responsible for writes and permissions. Files, network messages and imported packages are validated where they enter Lince.
 Lince's default visual identity is black and white by default, with purple as the supporting color. Purple Cobalt: #3730A3, Nocturnal Purple: #6366F1, Deep Lead: #121214, Gray: #A7B4C2, Ice White: #F8FAFC. Other themes are available in interface.

