There are several components made, here is the documentation on how to use them:

### IDE

A local text editor with a reusable File Explorer Castle. Rust handles directory listing and file operations directly. Loro retains edits and merges independent changes made on disk; competing changes require review. This is separate from Record sync and blob transfers. Opening a folder creates no File Records or database copies. Unsaved recovery drafts are stored locally beside the workspace.

Add the project folder, open a file, then open **Language tools**. Lince uses installed programs; connecting is manual and downloads nothing.

Install the tools you use:

- **NixOS or Linux with Nix:** `nix shell nixpkgs#bash-language-server nixpkgs#shfmt nixpkgs#shellcheck --command lince`. For permanent installation, add those packages to `environment.systemPackages` or Home Manager's `home.packages`. For Nix files, install `nixd nixfmt statix` instead. [Nix setup](https://wiki.nixos.org/wiki/Adding_programs_to_PATH)
- **Arch:** `sudo pacman -Syu bash-language-server shfmt shellcheck`.
- **Debian/Ubuntu:** `sudo apt install shfmt shellcheck`; with Node.js 20+ and npm, run `npm install --global --prefix "$HOME/.local" bash-language-server`, then `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`. [Server installation](https://github.com/bash-lsp/bash-language-server#installation)

Launch Lince from that terminal. From a configured checkout, use `cargo run -p lince`. With Nix, first enter `nix develop .#interface`, then use the tooling shell above with `--command cargo run -p lince`.

Check the prefilled fields for the open file's language:

| Language | Server | Formatter | Linter |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bash (`.sh`) | `["bash-language-server","start"]` | `["shfmt"]` | `["shellcheck","-"]` |
| Nix (`.nix`) | `["nixd"]` | `["nixfmt"]` | `["statix","check","--stdin"]` |

Fields are JSON lists: executable, then arguments. Use PATH names or full paths; `~`, `$HOME`, aliases and pipes are not expanded. `[]` disables a command. Connecting or running saves the settings. The deepest selected folder containing the file is its project root; otherwise its parent is used.

Click **Connect / Restart** and wait for Connected. **Ctrl+Space** requests completion; click a diagnostic to visit it. **Ctrl+Shift+I / Format** uses the server when supported, otherwise the formatter. **Format with command** always uses the formatter; **Run linter** shows its output. Formatting is one undo step and reaches disk through Save or autosave. **Disconnect** leaves normal editing and highlighting available.

Test with a small Bash file: expect a warning for unquoted `$1`, completion of a function name, and undoable formatting. Keep autosave off to check that only Save changes disk contents.

If a tool is missing, run `command -v bash-language-server shfmt shellcheck`, then restart Lince from that environment or enter the full path. An already running Lince does not inherit later environment changes.

Tools handle files up to 2 MiB, with four shared servers. Completion is plain text. Server settings currently use defaults; custom NixOS/Home Manager option completion may need settings the UI does not expose. [nixd settings](https://github.com/nix-community/nixd/blob/main/nixd/docs/configuration.md)

} r_99NWECF19VKV94HQQ3G3ASMC4M

Ideas (@ideas: 0) {
Things that wont enter v1.0.0, maybe later minor versions. Good to keep saved, not needed to maintain next to other tasks more certain to be implemented.

# Simulation:

Other Simulation follow-ups: broader Lingua declarations; controlled external responses, disk failures and crashes; more varied seeds and concurrent actions; blob contents saved with runs; real direct-connection tests across NATs, relays and mailboxes; focused Cell/event editing and Calendar links to explanations. Projection follow-ups include additional filters and permitted viewers, time boundaries, larger workloads and replacing older approximations where the shared runtime has coverage. General Record proposals, versions, history and transclusion should reuse the document machinery. Restore during a run and finer cache invalidation remain deferred until needed.

Simulation is the basis of controlling several Lince instances, each with their state. Karma runs on top of it, and the future changes of Records according to Karma's Rules are cached so we can display how a Record will recurringly have some state, like a quantity being some value, seen in a calendar.

- [ ]  Simulation not only of a random db, but the user's. If i have an appointment to day X i should be able to simulate it happening in day X+1. If i then send a scenario to someone, they can run that diff (like git's diff) and simulate a scenario of their db+diff. If its affecting only me i can simulate some state, doesnt need to be my current one, can be with a diff applied. If it affects others i send the diff, they simulate and aprove or not, they can also send that diff and a chain of approvals (or hide it) and get the results back before aproving themselves (if not before).
	- [ ] The Record Editor could also get the same 'simulation' which can actually be just a scenario of it writing something else, a version of a Record. How could we do it well? Not a frankestein of features for this?
	- [ ] It would be nice to be able to save different versions of the Record's description/title, or maybe quantity/link. Putting a limit to it in mb per total local history of edits, maybe keyed by amount of seconds spent not editing, makes a new save + explicit save. Again, how to not frankestein? Loro?
	- [ ] Be able to set Constraints, that are enforced, so the state doesnt reach an undesired point. Maybe this means putting a lot of constraints that need to be checked to make sure they are true in one run and not activating them in another, only a part of it. The one with less Constraints will be able to simulate more events per second happening since it doesnt need to be constantly checked to make sure it didnt pass thorugh an undesired state. Similar to the difference of unit tests and end-to-end ones. But instead of it being a division by part it is a division by period of Constraint checking, we can still have a difference of the equivalent of unit tests and e2e and several other types of tests, and still make them be checked in different times.
}

Ontology (@ontology: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #done) { r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F
Ontology in Lince can be a castle, it is for managing the content of your database. The meaning of records through Lingua Vocabulary, in the tasks it is referred as the central point for guaranteeing you have the data you want, dealing with sync and organ discovery, it is the vibe of several features in Lince. These are the remaining tasks of several features related to this Ontology vibe:

 [ ] Give long-range low-bandwidth radio (LoRa) its own transport adapter from the start.

 **Validation outside the local test environment:**
   - Android APK packaging and actual phone/emulator execution; library compilation already passes.
   - Actual screen-reader and visual review.
   - Internet deployment with always-online mailboxes and relays.
   - Power-loss durability; process interruption and SQLite rollback are already tested.

The agreed social Ontology features are implemented, including anonymous or identified Needs and Contributions, public profiles, conversation synchronization, introductions, contacts, discovery, consented gossip, saved searches, and offline mailboxes. The simple Karma correction and basic search optimization are also complete. The remaining work consists of practical validation, deferred features, and optional improvements.

Practical validation still needs to cover Android APK packaging and execution on a phone or emulator; Android library compilation already passes. The native interface needs visual review and actual screen-reader testing. Always-online mailboxes and Iroh relays need deployment and testing across real internet connections, including offline recipients, reconnection, and delivery after interruptions. Durability also needs testing against physical power loss; process interruption and SQLite rollback have already been tested locally. An optional live ACP-agent integration test remains unrun because it requires an installed, authenticated agent and a real model interaction.

LoRa remains a deferred implementation task. It needs its own transport adapter designed around radio’s limited bandwidth, small messages, and intermittent connections. This would provide another way for Cells to communicate where ordinary internet access is unavailable.

Broader work excluded from the completed social packages remains separate: Vocabulary publishing and importing, expanded Ontology and Relations exploration, completion of general file synchronization, Trail packages, Calendar export, and Blood integrations with other applications. Existing functionality in these areas should be reviewed before defining their remaining implementation tasks.

System push notifications while Lince is closed remain deferred; the implemented notification scope uses the existing in-app feed. A separate protocol for Lince servers to forward messages between one another also remains outside the chosen scope. Current communication uses Iroh for live connections and Lince mailboxes for asynchronous delivery. Dedicated backup development was removed from the plan: copying or archiving the complete Lince data directory while Lince is stopped remains the chosen approach.

Further search improvements are optional proposals. Large directory Organs could divide their collections by topic, language, or region while preserving publication consent, withdrawals, and security limits. In dispersed networks, consenting Organs could publish signed, expiring summaries of their collections to help people choose which sources to search. These summaries would guide searches without requiring a central registry or promising complete network coverage. Additional indexes should follow measurements showing expensive filter combinations. Progressive result display could make searches feel faster while slower selected sources are still answering. Current search already works locally and through explicitly selected helper Organs.

Other possible later social additions include group features, public comments, engagement ranking, and stronger protection against identifying people through connection metadata. Anonymous posting currently separates the public post from the person’s main Organ identity; stronger network anonymity would require additional design. These possibilities are not outstanding requirements of the completed six packages.
} r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F


